description:
[success] One Chat retirement (agents session e4). An Explore inventory came BEFORE any cut and classified about 120 hits into live, tests, migrations, docs, legit and false-positive. Then 3 parallel file-disjoint slices (agents code, SW code, docs). An independent reviewer in ANOTHER session (SW owner e1) reviewed the SW slice. e1 caught what the builder and orchestrator had missed:
(1) a live prod api_token row with the retired scopes, needing post-deploy revocation;
(2) the expand/contract deploy order. The agents image must stop calling the bridge before the SW image deletes the route, and the row-delete migration must come after the agents image, or the old image's ensure_default_agents re-seeds the row;
(3) keep the dead column instead of dropping it in the same release (old-image window, plus a separate owner go);
(4) the retired scope name was reused as a fake scope in 6 tests.
The independent reviewer had owned-domain context the builder lacked.

[measure] The adversarial security re-check after the compliance fixes found 1 P0 and 3 P1, plus 2 P2 and several P3. The hypothesis "no high remains after one re-check" is REFUTED for this slice.
What it caught that the compliance pass did not:
- it read WAHA's real API docs (start takes no body; markOnline defaults to true), where compliance reasoned only from our own code;
- it attacked nginx encoding semantics ($uri vs $request_uri, %3F);
- it looked OUTSIDE the diff (vps_exec's read-only path defeats every layer).
A Fake that emulated WAHA wrongly produced a false-green.
Refinements:
- re-checks on a security control loop until no P0/P1 remains, not "one pass";
- the brief must say "attack each layer assuming the others failed", plus "check the vendor's real docs" and "look outside the diff". This brief did, and it paid off;
- Fakes for vendor adapters need a vendor-docs parity check.
Cost: 247k tokens and 10 min, against the 413k fix round.

[failure] The guardian's `task_branch landed slug=agents` auto-cleanup removed the branch steward's long-lived integration worktree (.claude/worktrees/agents). The steward didn't know. Its next chained command `cd`'d into the missing dir, and the `git merge` then ran in the PRIMARY checkout on dev (1ed79cecf, local only, not pushed). The primary-write guard missed that chain, and then refused the reset-to-origin repair. Repair needs the owner. Cost: an owner round-trip. Lessons:
(1) landed cleanup must skip an integration branch whose steward is still delivering (b5 logged this);
(2) the steward should check that the dir exists before a chained cd, or use git -C with an absolute path;
(3) the guard gaps are logged as drift 691da3907.

[failure] D2b was ejected from the queue (1 of 2) after 7 build rounds and 7 security checks, because 3 defects sat OUTSIDE everything the loop judged:
- a meta MCP fixture using the seed WAHA client with no policy;
- test_deploy_verify broken by the vps.py change;
- a cross-delivery collision: agents stage 2's owner line sends via WAHA from every product, and D2b's fail-closed would refuse it.

Lesson: the adversarial loop optimises the slice's own guarantee and its own test set. A FAIL-CLOSED change to a SEED path breaks every caller, so before filing it needs:
(a) CI's exact job targets run locally (read them from .github/workflows, not a hand-picked scope);
(b) a caller census: grep every consumer of the changed seam across mcp/*, products/* and the seed framework;
(c) a re-check against whatever landed on dev in between.
This feeds the learn skill and the noc-steward pre-file checklist.

[success] An independent compliance review of backend-poller-fixes at d691a7916 (deliverable 5/5, every test red-first) found these problems:
- 1 blocker: the decision-log audit was blocked.
- 6 should-fix:
  - a lost-wake/lost-invalidation race in the new seed primitives (a send could be delayed 300s);
  - the clientes fill-if-empty write race;
  - clientes paging without an order, which silently skips rows above 1000;
  - the credential delete routes skip the cache invalidation;
  - the campaign due-query has no happy-path test;
  - a duplicate seed TTL cache.
It also cleared the main worry: cross-process staleness is moot, because each container runs a single uvicorn process.
